.TH "vsearch-allpairs_global" "1" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
vsearch --allpairs_global \[em] perform global pairwise alignments of
all sequence pairs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]vsearch\f[R] \f[B]--allpairs_global\f[R] \f[I]fastafile\f[R]
(\f[B]--acceptall\f[R] | \f[B]--id\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R])
(\f[B]--alnout\f[R] | \f[B]--blast6out\f[R] | \f[B]--fastapairs\f[R] |
\f[B]--matched\f[R] | \f[B]--notmatched\f[R] | \f[B]--qsegout\f[R] |
\f[B]--samout\f[R] | \f[B]--tsegout\f[R] | \f[B]--uc\f[R] |
\f[B]--userout\f[R]) \f[I]filename\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The vsearch command \f[V]--allpairs_global\f[R] performs optimal global
pairwise alignments (Needleman-Wunsch) for all pairs of sequences in a
fasta file.
Each sequence is compared to all sequences that follow it in the file,
for a total of n*(n-1)/2 comparisons where n is the number of sequences.
.PP
Sequences are compared on their \f[I]plus\f[R] strand only.
Either \f[V]--acceptall\f[R] (to write all alignments to output) or
\f[V]--id\f[R] (to set a minimum identity threshold) must be specified.
Many accept/reject options from the searching section also apply.
.PP
Masking is applied as specified with \f[V]--qmask\f[R] and
\f[V]--hardmask\f[R].
.PP
At least one output option must be specified.
This command is multi-threaded.
.PP
To illustrate the comparisons performed on a three-sequence file:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Input:    Pairs compared (n=3, total=3):

>s1       s1 vs s2
AAAA      s1 vs s3
>s2  -->  s2 vs s3
CCCC
>s3
TTTT
\f[R]
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.SS mandatory options
.PP
Either \f[V]--acceptall\f[R] or \f[V]--id\f[R] must be specified, along
with at least one output option.
.TP
\f[V]--acceptall\f[R]
Write the results of all pairwise alignments to output files.
Overrides all other accept/reject options, including \f[V]--id\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--id\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Reject the match if the pairwise identity with the target sequence is
lower than \f[I]real\f[R] (value ranging from 0.0 to 1.0 included).
The pairwise identity is defined by default as (matching columns) /
(alignment length - terminal gaps).
That definition can be modified with \f[V]--iddef\f[R].
.SS core options
.TP
\f[V]--iddef\f[R] \f[I]0|1|2|3|4\f[R]
Change the pairwise identity definition used with \f[V]--id\f[R].
Accepted values are:
.IP "0." 3
CD-HIT definition: (matching columns) / (shortest sequence length).
.IP "1." 3
edit distance: (matching columns) / (alignment length).
.IP "2." 3
edit distance excluding terminal gaps (default definition for
\f[V]--id\f[R]).
.IP "3." 3
Marine Biological Lab definition, counting each gap opening (internal or
terminal) as a single mismatch, whether or not the gap was extended: 1.0
- [(mismatches + gap openings)/(longest sequence length)].
.IP "4." 3
BLAST definition, equivalent to \f[V]--iddef 1\f[R] for global pairwise
alignments.
.TP
\f[V]--qmask\f[R] \f[I]none\f[R]|\f[I]dust\f[R]|\f[I]soft\f[R]
Mask regions in query sequences using the \f[I]dust\f[R] method or the
\f[I]soft\f[R] method, or \f[I]none\f[R] to suppress masking.
Values are case-insensitive, so \f[V]DUST\f[R], \f[V]Dust\f[R], and
\f[V]dust\f[R] are all accepted.
See \f[V]vsearch-fastx_mask(1)\f[R] for more details.
Warning, when using \f[I]soft\f[R] masking, search commands become case
sensitive: masking excludes masked regions from the \f[I]k\f[R]-mer
pre-filter that selects candidate targets (the pairwise alignment itself
always ignores case).
A query with no unmasked stretch of at least the word length samples no
\f[I]k\f[R]-mers and is therefore compared against every database
sequence.
The default is to mask using \f[I]dust\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--threads\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the number of computation threads to use, from 1 to 1024.
The number of threads should not exceed the number of available CPU
cores.
The value 0 is also accepted and, like the default, uses all available
cores; decimal values are truncated to their integer part.
On Linux, \[lq]available\[rq] accounts for the CPU affinity mask and the
cgroup CPU quota of the running process, so a job confined by
\f[B]taskset\f[R](1), Slurm, Docker or Kubernetes launches one thread
per core it was actually granted, rather than one per core the machine
has.
.SS secondary options
.TP
\f[V]--alnout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write pairwise global alignments to \f[I]filename\f[R] in a
human-readable format.
Use \f[V]--rowlen\f[R] to set the alignment line width.
.TP
\f[V]--blast6out\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write results to \f[I]filename\f[R] using a BLAST-like tab-separated
format with twelve fields per query-target match: query label, target
label, percentage identity, alignment length, mismatches, gap openings,
query start, query end, target start, target end, expectation value
(always -1), and bit score (always 0).
If \f[V]--output_no_hits\f[R] is used, non-matching queries are also
written.
Note that vsearch uses global pairwise alignments, not BLAST\[cq]s
seed-and-extend algorithm.
.TP
\f[V]--bzip2_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Huffman coding.
See \f[V]bzip2(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with bzip2,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--fasta_width\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the maximal width of sequences when writing fasta files.
Longer sequences are folded and written on several lines.
Default width is 80 nucleotides.
Set to zero (0) to suppress folding.
.TP
\f[V]--fastapairs\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write pairwise alignments of query and target sequences to
\f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta format.
.TP
\f[V]--gzip_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Lempel-Ziv coding.
See \f[V]gzip(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with gzip,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--hardmask\f[R]
Replace masked nucleotides with Ns.
.TP
\f[V]--idprefix\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the first \f[I]integer\f[R] nucleotides of
the target do not match the query.
.TP
\f[V]--idsuffix\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the last \f[I]integer\f[R] nucleotides of
the target do not match the query.
.TP
\f[V]--label_suffix\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the suffix \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers when writing fasta
or fastq files.
For example, with \f[V]--label_suffix \[dq];status=healthy\[dq]\f[R],
sequence header `>seq1' becomes `>seq1;status=healthy'.
.TP
\f[V]--leftjust\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the pairwise alignment begins with gaps.
.TP
\f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
Add a sequence length annotation (\f[V];length=integer\f[R]) to each
sequence header when writing fasta or fastq files.
.TP
\f[V]--log\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write messages to \f[I]filename\f[R].
Messages include program version, start and finish times, elapsed time,
amount of memory available, maximum amount of memory consumed, number of
cores and command line options, and if need be, command-specific
informational messages, warnings, and errors.
.TP
\f[V]--matched\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write query sequences matching a target sequence to \f[I]filename\f[R],
in fasta format.
.TP
\f[V]--maxdiffs\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the alignment contains more than
\f[I]integer\f[R] substitutions, insertions, or deletions.
.TP
\f[V]--maxgaps\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the alignment contains more than
\f[I]integer\f[R] gap openings: a run of consecutive gap columns counts
as one, whatever its length (use \f[V]--maxdiffs\f[R] to bound the
number of gap columns).
.TP
\f[V]--maxhits\f[R] \f[I]non-negative integer\f[R]
Set the maximum number of hits to report once the search is terminated
for a given query; hits are sorted by decreasing identity.
Unlimited by default, or when the argument is zero.
When searching both strands, \f[V]--maxhits\f[R] controls the total
number of hits reported per query across both strands.
.TP
\f[V]--maxid\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the pairwise identity between the two
sequences is greater than \f[I]real\f[R], a fraction from 0.0 to 1.0
(for example, \f[V]--maxid 0.97\f[R] rejects matches above 97%
identity).
.TP
\f[V]--maxqsize\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Reject query sequences with an abundance greater than \f[I]integer\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--maxqt\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the query/target sequence length ratio is
greater than \f[I]real\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--maxseqlength\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences longer than \f[I]positive integer\f[R] (50,000
nucleotides by default).
The value must not exceed 2,147,481,646 (\f[V]INT_MAX\f[R] minus 2,001).
.TP
\f[V]--maxsizeratio\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the query/target abundance ratio is greater
than \f[I]real\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--maxsl\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the shorter/longer sequence length ratio is
greater than \f[I]real\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--maxsubs\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the pairwise alignment contains more than
\f[I]integer\f[R] substitutions.
.TP
\f[V]--mid\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the pairwise identity, computed ignoring
all gaps (internal and terminal), is lower than \f[I]real\f[R], a
percentage from 0 to 100 (for example, \f[V]--mid 97\f[R] requires 97%
identity; note that the other identity options use fractions).
.TP
\f[V]--mincols\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the alignment length is shorter than
\f[I]integer\f[R] columns.
.TP
\f[V]--minqt\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the query/target sequence length ratio is
lower than \f[I]real\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--minseqlength\f[R] \f[I]non-negative integer\f[R]
Discard sequences shorter than \f[I]non-negative integer\f[R] (1
nucleotide by default).
A value of 0 retains empty sequences.
.TP
\f[V]--minsizeratio\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the query/target abundance ratio is lower
than \f[I]real\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--minsl\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the shorter/longer sequence length ratio is
lower than \f[I]real\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--mintsize\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Reject target sequences with an abundance lower than \f[I]integer\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--n_mismatch\f[R]
Count alignments of nucleotides against Ns as mismatches.
.TP
\f[V]--no_progress\f[R]
Suppress the gradually increasing progress indicator normally written to
the \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--notmatched\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the sequences that were not extracted to \f[I]filename\f[R], in
fasta format.
.TP
\f[V]--notrunclabels\f[R]
Retain whole sequence headers in output files.
By default, vsearch truncates sequence headers at first space or
tabulation.
This option suppresses truncation.
.TP
\f[V]--output_no_hits\f[R]
Write both matching and non-matching queries to \f[V]--alnout\f[R],
\f[V]--blast6out\f[R], \f[V]--samout\f[R], or \f[V]--userout\f[R] output
files.
Non-matching queries are labelled `No hits' in \f[V]--alnout\f[R] files.
.TP
\f[V]--qsegout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the aligned part of each query sequence to \f[I]filename\f[R], in
fasta format.
.TP
\f[V]--query_cov\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the fraction of the query aligned to the
target is lower than \f[I]real\f[R] (value ranging from 0.0 to 1.0
included).
Query coverage is computed as (matches + mismatches) / query sequence
length, not counting internal or terminal gaps.
.TP
\f[V]--quiet\f[R]
Suppress messages to the \f[I]standard output\f[R] \f[V]stdout(3)\f[R]
and \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R], except for warnings
and error messages.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Replace sequence headers with the prefix \f[I]string\f[R] and a ticker
(1, 2, 3, etc.).
For example, with \f[V]--relabel \[dq]cluster:\[dq]\f[R], the first
sequence header becomes `>cluster:1', the second sequence header becomes
`>cluster:2', and so on.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R]
Retain old sequence identifiers by including them at the end of the new
headers, after a space.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the MD5 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The MD5 digest is a 128-bit value (16 bytes), represented using a string
of 32 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]md5(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_self\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the sequence itself.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the SHA1 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The SHA1 digest is a 160-bit value (20 bytes), represented using a
string of 40 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]sha1(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--rightjust\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the pairwise alignment ends with gaps.
.TP
\f[V]--rowlen\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the width of alignment lines in \f[V]--alnout\f[R] output.
The default value is 64.
Set to 0 to disable line wrapping.
.TP
\f[V]--samheader\f[R]
Include header lines (\f[V]\[at]HD\f[R], \f[V]\[at]SQ\f[R],
\f[V]\[at]PG\f[R]) in the SAM file produced by \f[V]--samout\f[R].
By default, no header lines are written.
.TP
\f[V]--samout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write alignment results to \f[I]filename\f[R] in the SAM format, see
\f[V]vsearch-sam(5)\f[R].
Use \f[V]--samheader\f[R] to include header lines.
Each non-header line is a SAM record representing either a query-target
alignment or the absence of a match.
The alignment column of each record uses the CIGAR format, see
\f[V]vsearch-cigar(5)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--sample\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the given sample identifier \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers
when writing fasta or fastq files.
For instance, if \f[I]string\f[R] is `ABC', the text
\f[V];sample=ABC\f[R] will be added to the headers.
\f[I]string\f[R] is silently truncated at the first `;' or whitespace
character (space, tab, newline, carriage return, vertical tab or form
feed), so such characters should not be used in \f[I]string\f[R].
Other characters (alphabetical, numerical and punctuations) are
accepted.
.TP
\f[V]--self\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the query and target sequence labels are
identical.
.TP
\f[V]--selfid\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the query and target sequences are strictly
identical.
.TP
\f[V]--sizein\f[R]
Use the abundance annotations present in sequence headers when reading
fasta or fastq file.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Entries without abundance annotations are silently assumed to be of
\f[V]size=1\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--sizeout\f[R]
Add abundance annotations to sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Add the pattern \f[V];size=integer\f[R].
If option \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is not used, abundance values are set to 1
for all entries.
If \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is used, existing abundance annotations are simply
reported to output files.
.TP
\f[V]--target_cov\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Reject the sequence match if the fraction of the target sequence aligned
to the query is lower than \f[I]real\f[R].
Target coverage is computed as (matches + mismatches) / target sequence
length, not counting internal or terminal gaps.
.TP
\f[V]--top_hits_only\f[R]
Report only the hits with the highest pairwise identity for each query.
.TP
\f[V]--tsegout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the aligned part of each target sequence to \f[I]filename\f[R], in
fasta format.
.TP
\f[V]--uc\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write pairwise alignment results to \f[I]filename\f[R] in a
tab-separated uclust-like format with 10 columns.
One line is written per alignment (record type \f[V]H\f[R]); a query
with no accepted alignment produces a no-hit record instead (record type
\f[V]N\f[R]) \[em] the last sequence of the file always does, as it has
no following targets to be aligned with.
Columns are:
.RS
.IP " 1." 4
record type: \f[V]H\f[R] (alignment) or \f[V]N\f[R] (no alignment);
.IP " 2." 4
ordinal number of the target sequence (zero-based; \f[V]*\f[R] for N);
.IP " 3." 4
length of the query sequence (\f[V]*\f[R] for N);
.IP " 4." 4
percentage of identity with the target (\f[V]*\f[R] for N);
.IP " 5." 4
match orientation \f[V]+\f[R] (\f[V].\f[R] for N);
.IP " 6." 4
not used, always \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]*\f[R] for N);
.IP " 7." 4
not used, always \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]*\f[R] for N);
.IP " 8." 4
CIGAR alignment string (M, D, I; \f[V]=\f[R] if the query and target
sequences are strictly identical; \f[V]*\f[R] for N); see
\f[V]vsearch-cigar(5)\f[R];
.IP " 9." 4
query label;
.IP "10." 4
target label (\f[V]*\f[R] for N).
.RE
.TP
\f[V]--userfields\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Select and order the fields written to \f[V]--userout\f[R] output.
Fields are separated by \f[V]+\f[R] (e.g.\ \f[V]query+target+id\f[R]).
See \f[V]vsearch-userfields(7)\f[R] for a complete description of all
available fields.
.TP
\f[V]--userout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write user-defined tab-separated output to \f[I]filename\f[R].
Select and order the fields with \f[V]--userfields\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--xee\f[R]
Strip expected error (ee) annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]ee=float[;]\f[R].
Expected error annotations are added by the synonymous options
\f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R] and \f[V]--eeout\f[R] described in
\f[V]vsearch-fastx_filter(1)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--xlength\f[R]
Strip sequence length annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]length=integer[;]\f[R].
Sequence length annotations are added by the \f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
option.
.TP
\f[V]--xsize\f[R]
Strip abundance annotations from sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Abundance annotations are added by the \f[V]--sizeout\f[R] option.
.SS pairwise alignment options
.PP
These options modify the pairwise alignment scoring model.
Modify with caution.
.TP
\f[V]--gapext\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Set penalties for a gap extension.
See \f[V]vsearch-pairwise_alignment_parameters(7)\f[R] for a complete
description of the gap penalty declaration system.
By default, the penalty is set to 2 for extending internal gaps and to 1
for extending terminal gaps, in both query and target sequences.
.TP
\f[V]--gapopen\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Set penalties for a gap opening.
See \f[V]vsearch-pairwise_alignment_parameters(7)\f[R] for a complete
description of the gap penalty declaration system.
By default, the penalty is set to 20 for opening internal gaps and to 2
for opening terminal gaps, in both query and target sequences.
.TP
\f[V]--match\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Set the score assigned to a match (i.e.\ equivalent nucleotides) in
pairwise alignments.
The default value is 2.
Accepted values range from -32767 to +32767.
.TP
\f[V]--mismatch\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Set the score assigned to a mismatch (i.e.\ different nucleotides) in
pairwise alignments.
The default value is -4.
Accepted values range from -32767 to +32767.
.SS ignored options
.PP
These options are accepted for compatibility with usearch or with the
other search commands, but have no effect with
\f[V]--allpairs_global\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--band\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
This option is ignored.
It is provided for compatibility with usearch.
.TP
\f[V]--maxaccepts\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the maximum number of matching target sequences to accept before
stopping the search for a given query.
The default value is 1.
Use together with \f[V]--maxrejects\f[R].
If both \f[V]--maxaccepts\f[R] and \f[V]--maxrejects\f[R] are set to 0,
the complete database is searched.
Ignored: every following sequence is aligned, with no early termination.
.TP
\f[V]--maxrejects\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the maximum number of non-matching target sequences to consider
before stopping the search for a given query.
The default value is 32.
Use together with \f[V]--maxaccepts\f[R].
If both \f[V]--maxaccepts\f[R] and \f[V]--maxrejects\f[R] are set to 0,
the complete database is searched.
Ignored: every following sequence is aligned, with no early termination.
.TP
\f[V]--minwordmatches\f[R] \f[I]non-negative integer\f[R]
Set the minimum number of \f[I]k\f[R]-mer matches required for a
sequence to be considered further.
The default value is 12 for the default word length of 8.
If the argument is 0, no word matches are required.
Ignored: \f[V]--allpairs_global\f[R] has no \f[I]k\f[R]-mer
pre-filtering stage.
.TP
\f[V]--weak_id\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Report hits with a pairwise identity of at least \f[I]real\f[R], without
stopping the search.
Unlike \f[V]--id\f[R], weak hits do not count toward
\f[V]--maxaccepts\f[R] but do count toward \f[V]--maxrejects\f[R].
Values larger than the value specified with \f[V]--id\f[R] are silently
reduced to it.
With \f[V]--cluster_unoise\f[R], which does not use \f[V]--id\f[R],
\f[I]real\f[R] is the identity floor of the denoising step and defaults
to 0.90.
Ignored: only accepted alignments are reported; weak hits are never
written.
.TP
\f[V]--wordlength\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the length of words (i.e.\ \f[I]k\f[R]-mers) used for sequence
indexing and comparisons.
Valid values range from 3 to 15.
The default is 8.
Ignored: \f[V]--allpairs_global\f[R] builds no \f[I]k\f[R]-mer index.
.TP
\f[V]--fulldp\f[R]
This option is ignored.
It is provided for compatibility with usearch.
vsearch always uses a full dynamic programming algorithm
(Needleman-Wunsch).
.TP
\f[V]--hspw\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
This option is ignored.
It is provided for compatibility with usearch.
.TP
\f[V]--minhsp\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
This option is ignored.
It is provided for compatibility with usearch.
.TP
\f[V]--pattern\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
This option is ignored.
It is provided for compatibility with usearch.
.TP
\f[V]--slots\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
This option is ignored.
It is provided for compatibility with usearch.
.TP
\f[V]--xdrop_nw\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
This option is ignored.
It is provided for compatibility with usearch.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Compare all pairs in a fasta file and write alignments above 97%
identity to a BLAST-like tabular file:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --allpairs_global sequences.fasta \[rs]
    --id 0.97 \[rs]
    --blast6out results.tsv
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Write all pairwise alignments regardless of identity, using a
uclust-like tabular format:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --allpairs_global sequences.fasta \[rs]
    --acceptall \[rs]
    --uc results.uc
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Compare all pairs and write matching query sequences to a fasta file,
using 8 threads:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --allpairs_global sequences.fasta \[rs]
    --id 0.90 \[rs]
    --threads 8 \[rs]
    --matched matched.fasta \[rs]
    --notmatched unmatched.fasta
\f[R]
.fi
